  "summary": "A vibrant embroidery style known for its bright colors, gold thread work, and depiction of exotic themes including foreign subjects.",
  "description": "Yue embroidery features bold, contrasting colors and extensive use of gold and silver threads to create elaborate designs of dragons, phoenixes, and sometimes Western-influenced motifs. Developed in the cosmopolitan Canton region, it reflects the area's maritime trade connections and cultural exchange.",
  "culturalContext": "However, according to archaeological discoveries, it has been confirmed that there were inhabitants settled in Guangzhou region at least three to four thousand years ago. The area of present-day Yuexiu District was once the capital of the Nanyue Kingdom. Thirty-three years after the incorporation of Lingnan into the Qin Empire, Qin Shi Huang forced most of the Yue tribes out of Guangzhou and assimilated the rest. In 111 BCE, the area was reorganized as Panyu County, within the Nanhai Commandery. The area of present-day Yuexiu District served as the capital of both.\n\nThe area would serve as the seat of government for numerous successive administrative divisions, including Lingnan Circuit and Xingwang Fu (Chinese: 兴王府). It would continue to form part of Panyu County until 1921, when the it merged with Nanhai County to create Yuexiu District, and placed under the jurisdiction of the city of Guangzhou.",
